{"term": "عيب", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ʿayb", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "social_shame", "concept": "indecency", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 20, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 95, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A shameful act, defect, or social taboo; in sexual contexts, behavior that violates community norms of propriety and brings personal or familial disgrace.", "english_term": "shame", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Extremely common in everyday Arabic across all registers; used for anything from a social faux pas to serious sexual transgression; the sexual shame application is among its most culturally powerful uses."}
{"term": "فضيحة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "faḍīḥa", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_scandal", "concept": "slut_shaming", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 40, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A public scandal or disgrace, especially the exposure of a woman's sexual transgression that destroys her reputation and her family's honor.", "english_term": "sexual scandal", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "While faḍīḥa applies to any public scandal, its most consequential and culturally loaded application is the exposure of a woman's sexual behavior; fear of faḍīḥa is a primary driver of honor-based violence and family control of female sexuality."}
{"term": "عورة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ʿawra", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "nakedness_prohibition", "concept": "awra_islamic_nakedness", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 35, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The parts of the body that Islamic law requires to be covered; for men, the area between navel and knee; for most women, the entire body except face and hands; exposure of ʿawra is a religious violation rather than mere social impropriety.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Goes far beyond 'private parts' — ʿawra is a religiously defined category creating legal obligations of concealment that also governs whose nudity any given person may lawfully view, intersecting with maḥram rules; its extent is debated across legal schools."}
{"term": "ثأر الشرف", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "thaʾr al-sharaf", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "honor_vengeance", "concept": "arab_honor_vengeance", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 55, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Honor vengeance; retributive violence carried out to restore a family's sexual honor after a female member's transgression or rape, typically by killing her or her sexual partner.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Emphasizes the retributive-vengeance framing of honor violence, contrasting with jarīmat al-sharaf which uses neutral crime language; the thaʾr framing positions the killing as a legitimate moral duty rather than a crime, drawing on pre-Islamic blood-vengeance traditions absorbed into Arab honor culture."}
{"term": "جريمة الشرف", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "jarīmat al-sharaf", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "honor_killing", "concept": "honor_killing", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 60, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "An honor crime; a violent act, typically the murder of a female family member, committed to restore a family's damaged sexual honor after perceived or actual sexual transgression.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The formal Arabic legal-journalistic term, prominent in human rights reporting; English 'honor killing' is a widely-used calque but lacks a canonical registry entry; the crime framing reflects a modern legal lens on a practice that honor culture frames as a legitimate duty."}
{"term": "دنس الشرف", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "danas al-sharaf", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "honor_defilement", "concept": "arab_honor_defilement", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 45, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 48, "recognition_pct_peak": 48, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The defilement or pollution of family honor; the state of quasi-ritual disgrace that a woman's sexual transgression inflicts on her family's collective standing.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Danas (filth/defilement) combined with sharaf (honor) creates a rhetorically powerful compound evoking ritual contamination; more literary and rhetorical than everyday usage; the pollution framing maps honor damage onto the Islamic concept of najāsa."}
{"term": "سمعة المرأة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "sumʿat al-marʾa", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_sexual_reputation", "concept": "arab_female_sexual_reputation", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 15, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman's reputation, understood in Arabic honor culture as almost entirely determined by her perceived sexual propriety and chastity rather than by personal achievements or character.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "A noun phrase rather than a single lexeme; in Arabic honor-shame culture sumʿat al-marʾa is structurally coextensive with her sexual virtue — professional or intellectual accomplishments rarely constitute a woman's 'reputation' in this sense."}
{"term": "عفّة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ʿiffa", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "chastity", "concept": "sexual_abstinence", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Chastity and sexual virtue in Islamic ethics; the quality of restraining sexual desires within lawful bounds, regarded as a cardinal virtue especially for women but enjoined on all believers.", "english_term": "chastity", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Unlike English 'prudishness' (negative) or 'sexual abstinence' (neutral behavioral description), ʿiffa is an unambiguously positive moral virtue; it also encompasses modesty in speech, gaze, and dress beyond merely refraining from sex."}
{"term": "حياء", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ḥayāʾ", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_modesty", "concept": "islamic_sexual_modesty", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 87, "recognition_pct_peak": 87, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Modesty, bashfulness, and shame-consciousness as a positive Islamic virtue; in sexual contexts, the inhibition of displaying or discussing sexuality beyond prescribed norms, regarded as foundational to moral character.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "A prophetic hadith states 'al-ḥayāʾ min al-īmān' (modesty is a branch of faith), making it a core theological virtue; the closest English concept is 'prudishness' but that term carries entirely negative connotations that ḥayāʾ does not share."}
{"term": "خزي", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "khizī", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "humiliation", "concept": "indecency", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 35, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Disgrace, humiliation, or shame; in sexual contexts, the crushing public humiliation that befalls a family or individual as a result of sexual transgression becoming known.", "english_term": "disgrace", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "More literary and classical than ʿayb; used in Quranic language where it often connotes divine punishment and eternal shame; emphasizes the humiliating and crushing quality of the disgrace rather than the social-taboo dimension alone."}
{"term": "مسّ الشرف", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mass al-sharaf", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "honor_impugnment", "concept": "arab_honor_impugnment", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 30, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 50, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The 'touching' or impugnment of someone's honor; behavior, rumors, or accusations that begin to tarnish a family's sexual reputation without necessarily constituting full public scandal.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The euphemistic image of 'touching' (mass) implies incipient or partial violation rather than full defilement; appears in legal and journalistic contexts for lesser honor violations that may nonetheless trigger protective or retaliatory violence."}
{"term": "ولاية على المرأة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "wilāya ʿalā al-marʾa", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "guardianship", "concept": "islamic_male_guardianship", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The Islamic legal institution of male guardianship over women, granting male relatives authority over women's marriage, mobility, and sexual access; a central pillar of the honor-shame system controlling female sexuality.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Formally institutionalized in Saudi Arabia's guardianship system; contested by Muslim feminists; the institution structurally treats a woman's sexuality as a family and male prerogative rather than her own autonomous domain."}
{"term": "المحرم", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "al-maḥram", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "guardian_relative", "concept": "mahram_guardian", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A male relative so closely related to a woman by blood, nursing, or marriage that marrying her is permanently prohibited; in Islamic law, a maḥram must accompany a woman on travel and serves as her guardian and sexual protector.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "The prohibition of marriage between a woman and her maḥram defines precisely who may legitimately guard her — an intersection of sexual prohibition and sexual guardianship unique to Islamic jurisprudence; the maḥram list includes father, brother, son, paternal uncle, and men related through nursing."}
{"term": "ستر", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "sitr", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_concealment", "concept": "islamic_sexual_concealment", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The Islamic principle of concealing one's own and others' sins and sexual transgressions from public view; a positive religious virtue that prioritizes communal privacy over accountability for sexual misconduct.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Hadith encourages sitr: 'whoever conceals a Muslim's fault, Allah will conceal his fault on the Day of Judgment'; in practice this virtue has been invoked to suppress reporting of sexual abuse and rape within Muslim communities; related to but distinct from ʿawra (bodily concealment) — sitr is behavioral concealment."}
